# How Much Is Fusion Worth? Headroom, Floors, and the Reading of Multimodal Emotion Recognition Results

---

## Highlights

- Across **seven corpora and ten matched-architecture comparisons** — eight read from
  the published tables of five corpora, two measured here on two more — the gain of
  multimodal fusion over the best single modality ranges from **−1.8 to +9.1 points**.
  On four of the ten, fusion is *worse* than the best single channel. The ten rows are
  not ten independent experiments: MELD, AMIGOS and KEMDy20 each contribute two.
- The mainstream affective-computing benchmarks sit at the bottom of that range:
  MELD at 2.8–3.2, KEMDy20 at 0.8 on valence and −1.8 on arousal, AMIGOS negative on
  both dimensions.
- **CMU-MOSI and CMU-MOSEI, which dominate the missing-modality literature, sit there
  too.** Measured under one architecture with three fusion styles and five seeds, and
  with the fusion style chosen on held-out data rather than on test, MOSI gives −1.60
  accuracy and MOSEI +0.29. Held-out data cannot separate the two best styles on either
  corpus, and the other one gives −1.06 and +0.86. Either way: no gain on MOSI, under
  one point on MOSEI. On MOSI, audio-only and video-only both score below a constant
  classifier.
- A margin should be read as a fraction of the room available for it. A widely
  reported style of result — five points of weighted F1 under severe missingness —
  can be 70% of everything fusion is worth on that task.
- Comparing a deep fusion model against a shallow unimodal baseline inflates the
  apparent gain: on SEED-IV the same table yields 14.8 points read across
  architectures and 5.6 points read within one.
- Under a 48%-majority prior, a constant classifier scores 31.3 weighted F1 on MELD.
  We give the closed form, and show that the chance-corrected metrics — Cohen's kappa,
  multiclass MCC and balanced accuracy alike — provably cannot be raised by collapsing
  onto the prior, while weighted F1 rises under it in 97.6% of sampled cases.

---

## Abstract

Multimodal emotion recognition reports margins without reporting how much room those
margins occupy. We introduce two elementary diagnostics and measure them. The first is
the **fusion headroom** $H$, the gain of a paper's multimodal system over its own best
single modality, read from the same table under the same architecture. The second is
the **majority-class floor**, the score of a classifier that ignores its input, for
which we give closed forms. Reading eight matched comparisons from five corpora at
their source tables, and measuring two more ourselves on CMU-MOSI and CMU-MOSEI under
a single architecture — ten comparisons over seven corpora in all — we find $H$ between $-1.8$ and $+9.1$ points, with the
mainstream conversational, physiological and sentiment benchmarks at or below $3.2$
and four of the ten comparisons negative — fusing modalities makes those systems worse
than their best single channel. We show that mismatching architectures between the unimodal
and fused rows inflates $H$ by a factor of nearly three on one corpus, and that under
MELD's 48% neutral prior a constant classifier reaches 31.3 weighted F1, so that in
severely degraded conditions reported systems operate a few points above a model that
has learned nothing. We prove that kappa, MCC and balanced accuracy all fall under the
information-destroying operation that weighted F1 rewards in 97.6% of sampled
confusion matrices, and correct our own earlier claim that kappa was alone in this. A case study on a missing-modality framework we built to the
field's own standard shows the consequence: a 5.1-point advantage, 70% of that task's
entire headroom, that macro-F1 and kappa reduce to 0.6 and 2.5 points. We do not claim
published results are wrong; we claim the field reports a quantity whose scale it does
not report, and we give the three numbers that fix it.

**Keywords:** evaluation methodology; multimodal fusion; emotion recognition; missing
modalities; class imbalance; chance-corrected metrics

