## 1. Introduction

A multimodal emotion recognition paper reports that its system reaches 60.2 weighted
F1 where the previous best reached 59.2. The reader is invited to conclude that the
new fusion mechanism is better. What the reader is not told is that on that corpus,
under that paper's own architecture, using *all* the modalities instead of the single
best one is worth 3.2 points in total. The reported improvement is therefore not one
point out of forty; it is one point out of three.

This is not a criticism of any particular result. It is an observation about what is
missing from the table. Two quantities that bound the interpretation of every margin
in this literature are almost never reported:

**The floor.** MELD's test split is 48.1% neutral. A classifier that ignores its input
entirely and always answers *neutral* scores 31.3 weighted F1. Any cell below that
number describes a system that has not learned; any cell a few points above it
describes a system that has barely learned. In the single-modality and
severe-missingness conditions that robustness papers emphasise, reported scores are in
exactly that region.

**The headroom.** If a paper's own best single modality scores $s^\star$ and its full
multimodal system scores $f$, then $H = f - s^\star$ is everything that fusion is worth
in that paper's hands. A margin $\Delta$ over a competing system consumes $\Delta / H$
of it. When $H$ is three points, a claimed five-point margin is not a strong result; it
is an implausible one, and the first hypothesis should be that something other than
fusion produced it.

Neither quantity is difficult. The floor has a closed form; the headroom is a
subtraction within a table the paper already prints. Our contribution is to compute
them, at scale and at the source, and to show what they imply.

**What we do.**

1. We define $H$ and the reading discipline it requires — same paper, same table, same
   architecture — and show that violating the last of these inflates $H$ by nearly
   threefold on a corpus where the temptation is strongest (Section 3.2).
2. We read eight matched comparisons from five corpora at their source tables, measure
   two more ourselves on two further corpora, and report the range (Sections 4.2, 4.4).
3. We give closed forms for the majority-class floor and prove that the chance-corrected
   metrics — kappa, MCC and balanced accuracy — cannot be raised by collapse onto the
   prior, unlike weighted F1 (Section 5).
4. We audit our own missing-modality framework against these diagnostics, and report
   what they dissolve (Section 6).

**What we do not claim.** We do not re-run any published system, and we do not claim
any published number is incorrect. $H$ as we measure it is a property of a
*paper's systems*, not of the corpus in the abstract: a better fusion method could in
principle enlarge it. That objection is the substance of Section 7.1, and it is the
reason the paper is framed as a measurement rather than a bound.

