## 2. Related work

**Chance correction is old, and unapplied here.** Powers (2011) established that
recall, precision, F-measure and accuracy are biased, that a system worse in
Informedness can score higher under any of them, and that chance or base-case levels
must be identified. Chicco and Jurman (2020) argue for MCC over F1; Delgado and Tibau
(2019) argue against kappa in favour of MCC, a position Section 5.3 engages with
directly. None of this is new, and we claim none of it. What is new here is the
measurement: fifteen years after Powers, no missing-modality emotion-recognition paper
we examined reports a chance level for any degradation condition.

**Missing-modality emotion recognition.** The line divides into reconstruction —
imagining the absent channel (Zhao et al., 2021; Lian et al., 2023) — and reweighting,
which leaves it absent and adjusts the fusion (Zhang et al., 2024). Distillation from
a complete-modality teacher spans both (Li et al., 2024). Every one of these reports
margins under severe missingness; none reports the headroom those margins occupy.

**Evaluation audits in NLP.** Gorman and Bedrick (2019) showed that standard splits
change system rankings; Card et al. (2020) showed that much of the field is
statistically underpowered. This paper is of that genus: it measures a property of how
results are reported rather than proposing a method.

