## 3. Two diagnostics

### 3.1 Fusion headroom

For a paper reporting a multimodal system and unimodal ablations,

$$H \;=\; f \;-\; \max_{m} s_m ,$$

where $f$ is the score of the full multimodal system and $s_m$ the score of modality
$m$ alone, **all read from the same table, under the same architecture and protocol**.
$H$ is the total value of fusion as that paper realises it. A margin $\Delta$ between
two systems on that corpus has *utilisation* $\Delta/H$: the fraction of the available
room it claims.

$H$ is not a bound on what fusion could ever be worth. It is what fusion *is* worth to
the systems whose numbers the field compares against.

### 3.2 The reading discipline, and what breaks it

Three requirements, of which the third is the one that fails silently.

*Same paper and table.* Combining a unimodal score from one paper with a fused score
from another compares training regimes, not modalities.

*Same protocol.* Splits, label sets and metrics must match; $H$ computed across a
4-class and a 7-class protocol is meaningless.

*Same architecture.* This is the trap. Papers commonly report unimodal baselines with
a simple classifier and the fused system with a deep one. The difference then contains
both the fusion and the architecture, and attributing all of it to fusion inflates $H$.

**A worked instance.** Zheng et al. (2019) report, in one table on SEED-IV: EEG alone
70.33% and eye movements alone 67.82%, both with linear SVMs; feature-level fusion,
also with SVMs, 75.88%; and a bimodal deep autoencoder, 85.11%. Read against the deep
model, $H = 14.78$. Read within the SVM family, $H = 5.55$. The same table supports a
number nearly three times larger if the architectures are allowed to differ. We report
the matched value and record the other only as an illustration.

### 3.3 The floor

Let $c$ be a constant predictor emitting class $k_0$, whose share of the *evaluation*
set is $\pi_0$. Then

$$A(c) = \pi_0, \qquad
W(c) = \frac{2\pi_0^{2}}{1+\pi_0}, \qquad
M(c) = \frac{1}{K}\cdot\frac{2\pi_0}{1+\pi_0} .$$

*Proof.* Class $k_0$ has $\mathrm{TP} = n\pi_0$, $\mathrm{FP} = n(1-\pi_0)$ and
$\mathrm{FN} = 0$, so its precision is $\pi_0$, its recall is 1, and
$F_1(k_0) = 2\pi_0/(1+\pi_0)$. All other classes have $F_1 = 0$. Weighted F1 weights by
evaluation support, macro F1 averages over the label set. $\square$

Three hypotheses matter and are easy to lose. $\pi_0$ is the share of the class
*actually predicted* in the *evaluation* split, which is not the training prior's
maximum when the splits disagree — with training 60/40 and evaluation 70/30, predicting
the training majority scores 13.9 while the formula at the evaluation maximum would say
57.6. Macro F1's denominator is the label set, not the classes observed in the split;
`scikit-learn` uses the latter unless the label set is passed explicitly. And ties in
the prior leave the argmax non-unique, with the formula holding for whichever class is
emitted.

**Numerically.** MELD test ($K=7$, $\pi_0 = 0.481$): $W = 31.3$, $M = 9.3$,
$A = 48.1$. IEMOCAP four-class ($\pi_0 = 0.309$): $W = 14.6$, $M = 11.8$, $A = 30.9$.

