The range is $-1.8$ to $+9.1$ points. **Four of the ten comparisons are negative**:
fusing the modalities produces a system worse than its own best single channel. The two
rows we measured ourselves carry confidence intervals, and MOSI's contains zero — read
strictly, that row says fusion is worth nothing measurable there rather than that it
hurts.

The ordering is not the one the field's intuitions predict. EEG with eye movements —
two channels neither of which is derivable from the other — gives the largest headroom.
EEG with peripheral physiology, which shares that property, gives the smallest. Speech
with wearable biosignals, which we expected to be strongly complementary because
electrodermal activity is not a function of what is said, is negative on arousal.

The SGMT authors explain their own case, and the explanation generalises: acoustic
features already capture most of the arousal information in KEMDy20, and electrodermal
activity and inter-beat interval principally track arousal. The two channels overlap on
the dimension that carries the signal. Complementarity is not the absence of shared
content; it is the presence of *different* content on the *labelled* dimension.

### 4.3 What reading at the source changed

We first assembled this table from search summaries. Three entries were wrong.

**KEMDy20 was recorded at $H = 3.8$; it is $0.8$.** The paper's "+3.8 points" is
measured against its *speech-only* baseline at 49.0, not against the best single
modality, which for valence is bio-only at 52.0. On four of the six metrics in that
table, the fused system is below the best single channel.

**MELD was unresolved.** Search attributed the figure 56.44 to text-only in one place
and to the text+audio bimodal cell in another. Table 11 of the source settles it: 56.44
is bcLSTM text-only, and $H$ is 2.81 rather than the 6 we had estimated.

**SEED-IV was recorded at $H = 14.78$**, which is the cross-architecture reading of
Section 3.2.

Every correction moved the estimate downward. We report this because it is a fact about
how these numbers propagate: the summaries, the abstracts and the citing literature all
carry the larger figure.

### 4.4 A first-hand measurement: CMU-MOSI and CMU-MOSEI

**Why measured rather than read.** These two corpora carry most of the
missing-modality literature, so leaving them out would leave out the case the reader
cares about most. No table pairing unimodal with fused rows under one architecture was
reachable to us, and the community results file that was reachable lists multimodal
systems only. Rather than record numbers from memory or from a summary — the practice
Section 4.3 shows failing three times out of three — we ran the comparison.

**Data.** Utterance-level features and speaker-independent splits from the bc-LSTM line
of work (Poria et al., 2017; Poria et al., 2017b) are committed in a public repository at
a fixed commit; we record the commit and the per-file checksums. The MOSI features are
the ones released with that ACL 2017 paper. CMU-MOSEI itself is later — Zadeh et al.
(2018) — so its features in that repository postdate the 2017 work and we could not
verify the provenance of each pickle independently; the MOSEI row is therefore a
measurement on a specific published feature distribution, not a reproduction of any
paper's pipeline.
MOSI is binary sentiment, 1,447 training and 752 test utterances, with text at 100
dimensions, audio at 73 and video at 100. MOSEI is three-class sentiment, 16,188 and
4,614 utterances, all three channels at 100 dimensions. Labels and validity masks are
identical across the three modality files, which is what makes the unimodal and fused
rows matched at the sample level rather than only in aggregate; the loader asserts it.

**Protocol.** One architecture throughout: a masked bidirectional LSTM over the
utterances of a video with a per-utterance linear read-out, sequences packed so that
the backward pass never runs over padding. Optimiser, schedule, early stopping, seeds
and read-out are held fixed. The only thing that varies between configurations is which
feature blocks are fed in.

Two decisions guard against handicapping fusion, which would manufacture our own
conclusion. First, fusion is given **three** chances, not one: early concatenation, late
averaging of the unimodal posteriors, and per-modality encoders concatenated before the
read-out; $H$ is taken against the best of the three. Which style counts as best — and
which single modality — is decided on the held-out training videos, never on test:
taking the maximum of test means is a post-hoc selection that inflates $H$, and an
earlier version of this section did exactly that (the correction is reported below).
Second, hyper-parameters are
selected per configuration on held-out data over one shared grid, because handing the
fused model the unimodal models' width would penalise an input three times as wide. We
report the fixed-hyper-parameter protocol as primary and the selected one as a
robustness check.

