## 6. Case study: what the diagnostics do to a method paper

We built a missing-modality framework to the standard the field uses: per-channel
evidential heads, a fusion gate weighted by Dirichlet vacuity, and asymmetric
distillation from a complete-modality teacher, evaluated on a controlled benchmark with
MELD's class priors and split sizes, over ten seeds and seven availability patterns,
with paired significance tests. It reported an advantage of **+5.06 weighted F1** over
the strongest baseline in the visual-only condition.

The diagnostics of this paper, applied afterwards:

- **Headroom.** $H = 7.3$ on that benchmark. The claimed margin is **69% of everything
  fusion is worth** on the task. That should have stopped the experiment at design time.
- **Floor.** The system scores 36.0 in the visual-only condition, against a floor of
  31.0. Four of the six systems compared score *below* the floor in that condition; the
  margin measures which system collapses onto the prior less completely.
- **Chance correction.** Against the strongest baseline: $+1.04$ weighted F1
  ($p = 0.10$), $+0.52$ kappa ($p = 0.30$). The 5.06-point visual-only margin becomes
  $+0.66$ macro-F1 and $+2.47$ kappa. *All kappa differences in this section are
  reported as $100 \times \Delta\kappa$, on the same scale as the F1 differences beside
  them; $+2.47$ means $\Delta\kappa = 0.0247$. Kappa itself lies in $[-1, 1]$.*

One result survives all three: against a matched-capacity attention gate, the advantage
is $+2.19$ weighted F1 ($p = 0.0095$), $+1.97$ kappa ($p = 0.0107$) and $+1.84$ MCC
($p = 0.0077$). That is what a margin looks like when it is not an artifact of the
metric — smaller, and consistent across metrics that disagree about what to reward.

We report this case study on our own work rather than on someone else's deliberately.
The framework was built competently, evaluated more carefully than is typical, and
still produced a headline number that three elementary checks dissolve.

---

## 7. Limitations

### 7.1 $H$ is a property of systems, not of corpora

This is the substantive objection. What we measure is the headroom *realised by the
systems in the papers we read*, not an information-theoretic bound. A better fusion
method could enlarge it, and a paper reporting weak unimodal baselines will appear to
have more of it.

Three things temper this without removing it. The corpora we read span eleven years and
very different architectures — SVMs with fuzzy integrals, contextual LSTMs, gated
transformers — and the range does not widen with model capacity. The direction of the
error from weak unimodal baselines inflates $H$, so the small values we report are, if
anything, upper estimates. And the quantity is the right one for the purpose: a margin
is claimed against the systems the field actually compares, so the room available is the
room those systems leave.

Section 4.5 tests the second of these on one row. Re-extracted with frozen modern
encoders, MELD's text-only baseline rises by about five points of weighted F1 and $H$
falls from 2.81 to 0.41: the published headroom was, in part, room left by the unimodal
baseline. Still, a genuinely stronger fusion method on MELD would falsify the reading of
that row, and we would welcome it.

### 7.2 One paper per corpus, and metric heterogeneity

MELD supplies two rows that differ by 0.4 points, which is reassuring but is a sample of
two. The other corpora supply one each. The metrics differ — accuracy, weighted F1,
UAR, F1 — and $H$ values are not comparable in absolute terms across them; the claim is
about the range within each, not about ranking corpora against one another.

### 7.3 DEAP is missing, and it is not missing at random

Its tables are images in the published PDF. A corpus we could not read is a corpus we
could not include, and we cannot rule out that unreadable tables differ systematically
from readable ones.

### 7.4 Eight rows are read, two are reproduced

The eight published rows are read, not reproduced. Reading resolves attribution errors,
as Section 4.3 shows, but it inherits every error in the source. Section 4.5 re-measures
one of them on different features and, for that reason, leaves it in the table as read.

The two rows we measured have the opposite exposure, and one specific weakness worth
stating plainly. The released text and video features were extracted by networks trained
on the training split: held-out *training* videos reach 98.8% and 94% accuracy where the
test split reaches 80.5% and 54%, while the audio features, which are unsupervised
acoustic descriptors, show no such gap. The consequence is that the validation signal is
saturated, so per-configuration hyper-parameter selection is weaker than it looks — which
is why we report the fixed-hyper-parameter protocol as primary and why both protocols
appear. The same saturation limits the choice of fusion style, which Section 4.4 now
makes on held-out data: on MOSI, text and all three fusion styles reach 98.0–99.4%
held-out accuracy against 78–81% on test, so that choice is made among near-ties, and
the sign of MOSI's $H$ should not be read as more than "no gain". It does not break the match between rows, since all configurations share one
feature set, but it does mean our MOSI and MOSEI numbers describe fusion *over these
features*. The same release's IEMOCAP features show the contamination directly: its text
features were fit on the labels of four of the five sessions, and replacing them with
any of three frozen encoders that never saw a label turns a 31-point accuracy gap between
two held-out sessions into a 7-point gap in the opposite direction (the repository's
`docs/ro-ri-dac-trung-iemocap.md`). A stronger claim would need modern text, speech and
vision encoders on MOSI and MOSEI as well, and we do not make it.

---

## 8. Recommendations

For each degradation or ablation condition, three numbers alongside the metric the
field expects:

1. **The floor** for that split, $2\pi_0^2/(1+\pi_0)$, from the label counts.
2. **The headroom** $H$, matched on paper, table, protocol and architecture — and, when
   comparing against prior work, the utilisation $\Delta/H$ of the claimed margin.
3. **A chance-corrected metric** — Cohen's kappa, multiclass MCC or balanced accuracy —
   none of which a collapse onto the prior can raise. Report kappa and MCC together
   near the floor: kappa is the stabler estimate, MCC the more sensitive one
   (Section 5.3).

A margin that exists in the field's metric and vanishes in the third has not been shown
to be a robustness gain. A margin whose utilisation exceeds half the available headroom
deserves the author's suspicion before the reviewer's.

---

## 9. Conclusion

The field reports margins without reporting the scale they are measured on. We measured
that scale. Across seven corpora and ten matched comparisons, using every modality a
paper's own table reports — which on the MELD rows means text and audio, not video —
instead of the best single one is worth between $-1.8$ and $+9.1$ points, the
mainstream benchmarks sit at or below $3.2$, and on four comparisons fusion makes the
system worse than its own best channel. The two corpora that carry the
missing-modality literature are among the smallest: measured under one architecture
with the fusion style chosen on held-out data, MOSEI yields between $+0.29$ and
$+0.86$ accuracy — held-out data cannot separate the two styles that give them — and
MOSI between $-1.60$ and $-1.06$. Under a 48%-majority prior a model that ignores
its input scores 31.3 weighted F1, and in the severely degraded conditions this
literature emphasises, systems operate a few points above it.

None of this says that multimodal fusion does not work. It says that what it is worth,
on the corpora the field uses and in the hands of the systems the field compares, is
small — and that a metric which rewards collapse onto the class prior is the wrong
instrument for measuring small things. Our own framework produced a five-point headline
inside seven points of headroom; three checks that cost nothing reduced it to
six-tenths of a point of macro-F1. We expect the same arithmetic applies more widely
than to us, and the three numbers in Section 8 are enough for any author to find out.

**Future work.** Two directions, neither requiring a new architecture. Repeat the
measurement of Section 4.4 with frozen modern text, speech and vision encoders, which is
the one reading of our MOSI and MOSEI rows we cannot currently rule out — that the small
headroom is a property of 2017-era features rather than of the corpora. The encoders
must stay frozen: fine-tuning them on benchmark labels and then extracting features for
every split is how the released features came to be contaminated, and fusing clean
channels with contaminated ones can manufacture a finding of its own. And ask the
question the headroom range raises: if the value of fusion is a few points on these corpora, what corpus design
would make it large — which is a question about how emotion is expressed across
channels, not about architectures.

